WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.812
The printer completes its programmed path. A moment

00:00:03.812 --> 00:00:07.595
later the edible structure sags. The code was correct;

00:00:07.595 --> 00:00:09.696
the material did something else.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.089
A printer can follow a perfect toolpath while the

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:24.324
deposited material collapses. The rheology of the ink

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:25.537
sets the rules.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.120
The printing material must move through a nozzle, then

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.383
retain enough structure after deposition to realise the

00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:41.199
intended form.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.610
The forthcoming chapter considers three-, four-, five-

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.582
and six-dimensional food-printing concepts, including

00:00:55.582 --> 00:00:58.554
responsive structures and programmed behaviour.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.935
Composition, texture and printing conditions interact.

00:01:06.935 --> 00:01:10.420
A promising design requires material-specific testing

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.324
before its properties are asserted.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.731
Rheology sets the boundary between possible shapes and

00:01:22.731 --> 00:01:25.941
imagined ones. Different dimensional labels must

00:01:25.941 --> 00:01:28.617
correspond to demonstrable functional behaviour.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.189
Time-responsive and higher-dimensional labels describe

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:39.573
proposed capabilities; they do not establish that every

00:01:39.573 --> 00:01:42.312
structure has been practically demonstrated.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.635
Programmable edible structures start with a material

00:01:49.635 --> 00:01:53.276
that can survive deposition, not with an impressive

00:01:53.276 --> 00:01:53.731
rendering.
